Golden fixtures and runner for Prosper's remote frames protocol 1.0, copied
from ventsislav-georgiev/prosper 96b65ad (docs/protocol/golden/). The spec is
docs/protocol/remote-frames.md there. Fixtures 01-15 and run.py are Prosper's;
re-copy them from a newer Prosper commit and update the commit named here,
then redo the dch change below.

dch has changed one Prosper file since the copy:
- golden/run.py: default --timeout 5 -> 10 (#404, 1.80.0). 5 s cut off 34
  against its 6 s setup session on a loaded runner.

Fixtures 17 and up are dch's own, so dch edits them freely. Changed since
they were added: 18-26, 33, 34, 36-38 lost the expected FIN after a 0x33 (#118,
the connection stays open); 32 changed with X-ray (1.55.0); 33's setup
session sleeps 6 s, not 2 (#404). 34 (--follow) needs the 10 s default
--timeout: `run.py --timeout 5` fails it.

They are in the same format (#114): the "ctl" op of
remote frames 1.1 (0x30 request; 0x31 stdout, 0x32 stderr, 0x33 exit). They
need "ctl" in ops; 40-43 (#118: 0x34 cancel, warm reuse, busy, the idle
close) need "ctl-cancel". Two keys are dch's: "dch_serve" ({"args",
"env"}) names how the server must start (create off, a lowered cap, a
lowered timeout, a lowered idle limit);
those fixtures require the op "dch-serve-config", which no server names, so
a plain run.py run skips them. "dch_mirror" marks a fixture for the full
build only (--read, --keys and --follow need the terminal mirror).

tests/serve_test.py runs them against `dch serve` with --mutating, each
"dch_serve" group against a dch serve of its own.
